Z||000001||StuGood||01-28-2001||08:36 PM||||We wish we had more uncles like yours  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> !<P>Crude control    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> problems can be due to a number of things.  Start here: <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/cruise-control.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/cruise-control.html</A>  <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/diag.cruise-control.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/diag.cruise-control.html</A> <BR>Good luck    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-28-2001||08:50 PM|||| The very first thing I would do is check the fuses. Sounds stupid but you don't know how many people never think of this. Next I would check your vacuum line connections to the Servo motor and such. <P>There are two release switches, one on the brake pedal and the other on the clutch arm if you have a manual transmission. You might want to check these switches because if they are faulty than the cruise control will not engage. There is also a controller (small computer) that is the brain of the whole system and that controller uses inputs from the vehicle speed sensor that I think is located inside the speedometer gauges. You will have to get a manual to check that stuff.<P>You sick of me yet? <P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
